<% content_for :title, "Terms" %>

<div class="l-container">
  <h1><%= multicolor("Terms") %></h1>

  <p class="m-lede">
    Short version: your quartets are yours. You keep full ownership of every
    puzzle you make here. We take the bare minimum permission needed to run the
    site, and not a drop more.
  </p>

  <h2>Your quartets are yours</h2>
  <p>
    You own the copyright in every quartet you create — the categories, the word
    picks, the descriptions, all of it. Saving or publishing here transfers
    nothing. We don't own your puzzles, we can't sell your puzzles, and we don't
    relicense them to anyone. This is true whether you have an account or made
    your quartet anonymously.
  </p>

  <h2>The one permission you give us</h2>
  <p>
    By saving a quartet on Quartets you grant us a <strong>non-exclusive,
    royalty-free license</strong> to host, store, display, and share it — solely
    so the site can do its job: show your puzzle to the people you share it
    with, list it in the archive if you publish it, render its share card, and
    keep its stats. That's the entire grant. It ends when you delete the puzzle.
    (Anonymous, aggregate play stats survive a deletion — those describe the
    plays, not the puzzle.)
  </p>

  <h2>What everyone else gets</h2>
  <p>
    They get to play it. That's it. Publishing a quartet does <em>not</em> put
    it under an open license — nobody may copy, republish, or sell your puzzle
    without your say-so. If you <em>want</em> your work to be remixable, that's
    your right to give away, wherever and however you like.
  </p>

  <h2>Our own content</h2>
  <p>
    The site's code is <%= link_to "MIT", "https://github.com/johnhutch/quartets/blob/main/LICENSE", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>;
    the content <em>we</em> wrote — our own puzzles, the site copy, the docs —
    is <%= link_to "CC BY 4.0", "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
    Those licenses cover our work only, never yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Ground rules</h2>
  <p>
    Only publish what's yours to publish. Don't post someone else's puzzle,
    hateful junk, or spam. Our moderators can unpublish or remove content that
    breaks these rules — that's the whole extent of our editorial power, and
    removing a puzzle never touches your ownership of it.
  </p>

  <h2>If someone stole your work</h2>
  <p>
    If a quartet on this site lifts your work — or someone lifted yours — tell
    us at <%= link_to "johnhutch.com", "https://johnhutch.com", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>
    and we'll sort it out. Usually that means taking the copy down.
  </p>

  <p><%= link_to "← Back to the quartets", root_path %></p>
</div>
